{ println } :: import("std/fmt");
// Every assignment emits a "Save old value for later use" temp before the
// store, whether or not the old value will ever be dropped. Inspect with:
//
//   yo compile <this file> --optimize 2 --emit-c --skip-c-compiler
//   grep -c 'Save old value' <out>.c        # 8, for the four lines below
//
// The saves below are all DEAD — each temp is declared once and never read.
// For an RC type the same save IS used, feeding __yo_decr_rc, which is why it
// exists; the gate on "does this old value need dropping" is what is missing.
//
// The sharper case is std/prelude's `Array(T, U).default()`, which writes into
// MaybeUninit storage: there the dead save READS INDETERMINATE MEMORY.
//   a := Array(i32, usize(4)).default();
main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  a := Array(i32, usize(4)).fill(i32(7));
  (i : usize) = usize(0);
  while(i < usize(4), {
    a(i) = i32(1);
    i = (i + usize(1));
  });
  println(`${a(usize(0))}`);
});
export(main);
